WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning John!

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.620
Last Sunday, tens of thousands of people took
to the streets in Hong Kong to protest.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.320
The police reactions to these protests were,
surprisingly, intense - resulting in, get

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.519
this, far more people joining the protest!

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:13.249
This somewhat surprising result is only one
of the things that can be explained through

00:00:13.249 --> 00:00:17.281
a better understanding of what Hong Kong is,
what those people are protesting about, and

00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:18.990
what is motivating China.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.290
A brief history!

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.290
-

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.150
1847: Hong Kong, a sparsely populated island
off the southern coast of China, is ceded

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.000
to the United Kingdom after the Opium War.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.930
1898: China and Hong Kong agree to a 99 year
lease of Hong Kong to the British Empire to

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.110
be a British Colony.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.110
99 years!

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:37.329
That's, that's plenty of time to figure out
- NOPE.

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:41.320
In the 90's, with the switch impending, the
appointed - NOT elected - Governor of Hong

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.270
Kong started to talk about how it would be
really great if Hong Kong were a democracy.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.110
This is kinda hilarious after 150 years of
colonial rule.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.380
China finds this, not just hilarious, but
also really annoying because China would like

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:58.960
to have Hong Kong's extraordinarily economically
important cake and also eat it.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.170
1997: after 150 years of British rule, Hong
Kong becomes part of China.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.170
Kind of.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.850
After 150 years, Hong Kong has its own dialect,
and judiciary, and taxes, and postal service,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.100
and money, and culture!

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.650
People from Hong Kong are ethnically Chinese,
and they feel connected to the Chinese people,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.100
but they don't consider themselves Chinese.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.820
To get an idea of how weird this is, if you
wanted to go from China, to China, but you

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.180
happened to be crossing the Hong Kong border,
you have to get your passport stamped and

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.180
go through immigration.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.869
To get a better idea of how weird this is,
during the 2008 Beijing Olympics, both China

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:30.600
and Hong Kong had separate teams...

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.600
Same country!

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.500
At the root of it, this is all because what
Hong Kong is now is really great for China.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.570
Not only would it be extremely difficult to
have Hong Kong run by the same system as China,

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.260
which would be COMPLETELY different from the
entire culture of Hong Kong, it would also

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.470
be bad for China!

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.621
China wants Hong Kong to be the thing that
it is.

00:01:47.621 --> 00:01:53.130
It's a capitalist powerhouse that was created
as a bridge between China and the capitalist

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.130
world.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.350
Hong Kong houses the third largest stock exchange
in the world; the Hong Kong dollar is the

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.090
eighth most traded currency in the world!

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:02.939
For all that to keep working, Hong Kong kinda
has to be a thing that China finds really

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:04.300
uncomfortable - Free.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.080
They call it, "one country, two systems."

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.400
Alright, this might be a little bit of a difficult
thing to understand.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.780
In America, we make fun of a politician on
TV.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.790
That's not just okay, it's also, like, if
that politician can handle it, it's kind of

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.630
a good thing for them - it makes them seem
more powerful.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.400
In China, where everything is very tightly
controlled, that kind of criticism would make

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.210
a politician seem extremely weak.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.480
So you have to imagine China as being run
by a culture that does not accept any kind

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.489
of derision or appearance of weakness.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:33.810
They're crazy with the control.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:37.750
China's national day was this week and they
let loose ten thousand doves in Tiananmen

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:43.239
Square - they cavity searched EVERY dove to
make sure there were no explosives inside

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:44.239
of them.

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:45.239
That is what you think it is.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:51.020
China's government attitude is a mixture of
immense pride in what they have done and vulnerability

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.760
- just fear of it falling apart.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.650
And that might be somewhat warranted, because
there are lots of China that don't want to

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.870
be areas of China!

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.769
And with a weakening economy and the number
of protests in China growing, politicians

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:07.030
are worried about losing stability - and they
REALLY like stability.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.590
Now add, to all of this, that the current
president of China, Xi Jingping, just did

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.640
a massive crackdown on corruption.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.269
This made him a lot of enemies of the Chinese
officials who were benefiting from corruption.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.580
You have a situation where the leadership
in Beijing really wants to appear strong and

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.640
in control and not at all weak, and is thus
not in any way interested to conceding to

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.540
the protests in Hong Kong, or even allowing
them to continue!

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.510
The protest, itself, does probably hurt Beijing
- though maybe not as much as they imagine

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.260
because people in Hong Kong and people in
China don't actually get along that well anymore

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.970
for reasons that, surprisingly, involve shopping
excursions.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.959
Now I've gotten a long way into this and I
haven't even talked about the reason why people

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:44.870
started protesting last Sunday.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.910
And that's kind of because, with all this
background, it starts to feel... inevitable.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.320
Like it's just a thing that was gonna happen.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.630
But here's the specifics - since the hand-off
of Hong Kong to China, it has been run, not

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:57.940
by a Governor or a mayor, but a Chief Executive
if it wasn't capitalist enough for you.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.909
The Chief Executive is elected by a kind of,
like, weird electoral college composed mostly

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:06.000
of, like, business tycoons that represent
different constituencies, mostly corporate

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.000
constituencies.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.460
Now, Hong Kong's constitution, which isn't
really a constitution, eventually calls for

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.830
the election of the chief executive by the
people, and not by a group of twelve hundred

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.799
people who have the exact same interests as
Beijing.

00:04:15.799 --> 00:04:19.229
But, Beijing interprets that law a little
bit differently, saying that, yes, the citizens

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:24.539
of Hong Kong can elect their Chief Executive,
but they have to choose from a pool of candidates

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:26.779
that Beijing pre-selects for them.

00:04:26.779 --> 00:04:28.530
And that, of course, is not a democracy.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.139
Now maybe even more importantly, since the
1989 massacre at Tiananmen Square, the people

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.999
of Hong Kong have had a proud and strong history
of demonstration and protest.

00:04:35.999 --> 00:04:40.130
And the intense response from the Hong Kong
police was maybe even more scary to the people

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.819
of Hong Kong than was the prospect of not
being able to elect their own leaders - because

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:46.599
the people of Hong Kong have never been able
to elect their own leaders but they HAVE been

00:04:46.599 --> 00:04:48.300
able to freely protest.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.659
Some people hope that, as Hong Kong was a
testing ground for China's unique form of

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:56.080
capitalism, it might also become a testing
ground for actual Chinese democracy.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.279
But the cultural gap between the structure
and control of China and the freedom of Hong

00:05:01.279 --> 00:05:03.309
Kong may be too big to bridge.

00:05:03.309 --> 00:05:07.449
But while the protests are bad for the image
of Chinese leaders, quashing them would be

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:10.210
bad for the continued economic prosperity
of Hong Kong.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.599
And the protesters know that they shouldn't
mess too much with Beijing, that's why they're

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:16.120
directing most of their ire at the Chief Executive.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.509
China's state media, meanwhile, does report
on the protests in Hong Kong, just like they

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:21.460
reported on the protests of the Arab Spring.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.839
But, as they did with the Arab Spring, they
focus on the chaos and inconvenience that

00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:27.259
these protests bring.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:31.389
And, really, what China needs is to stick
together and be stable.

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:33.150
Yay, stability!

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:37.470
And with the protesters in Hong Kong protesting
in very calm and stable ways, there seems

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.719
to be a certain amount of agreement that stability
is good.

00:05:39.719 --> 00:05:44.059
There aren't very many voices calling for
Hong Kong to secede from China, though China

00:05:44.059 --> 00:05:48.500
may be afraid that, with continued cultural
changes, that may eventually be the case.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.669
But mostly, Hong Kong just wants to be Hong
Kong - and exceptional, unique place where

00:05:52.669 --> 00:05:54.069
lots of amazing things happen.

00:05:54.069 --> 00:05:58.500
Just, ideally, a Hong Kong with a little bit
more control over its own destiny for, really,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.069
the first time in its entire existence.

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:04.089
But in the country where they check the butts'
of pigeons for explosives, having an entire

00:06:04.089 --> 00:06:11.060
seven million person part of the country not
controlled by the central government, is maybe

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.889
not just scary, it might just be a completely
foreign concept.

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:18.880
But just as China found ways to allow actual
capitalism to occur within its borders, maybe

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.469
there's a way for actual democracy to occur
within its borders.

00:06:22.469 --> 00:06:26.490
That would be pretty amazing, and that would
be a pretty fantastic outcome to this very,

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.649
very weird story.

00:06:27.649 --> 00:06:28.749
I guess we'll see.

00:06:28.749 --> 00:06:30.649
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

